<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Observed Strategies ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> depicts the alternation rate, which measures how frequently the proposer switches between the two players from one proposal to the next. Only the two commercial GPT reasoning models (GPT-5 and GPT Mini) achieve a perfect alternation rate of <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> across all languages and experiments. These two models are also the best on our game. The alternation rate is defined as the ratio of consecutive proposals where a different player makes the offer, ranging from 0 (one player proposes every time) to 1 (they perfectly take turns).
Let <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i})_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i})_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be time-ordered proposals, and let <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(i)\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(i)\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the proposer of <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{alt}\;=\;\frac{1}{\,n-1\,}\sum_{i=2}^{n}\mathbf{1}\!\left\{\,b(i)\neq b(i-1)\,\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>alt</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{alt}\;=\;\frac{1}{\,n-1\,}\sum_{i=2}^{n}\mathbf{1}\!\left\{\,b(i)\neq b(i-1)\,\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the rate is undefined.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Collaboration</h5>

<figure id="A4.F45" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08098v1/substitutions_dual_all_GPT-5.svg" id="A4.F45.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 45: </span>Substitutions over proposals in temporal order for GPT-5.
Values are averaged over proposals at each time step in a negotiation across all experiments and languages.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F46" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08098v1/substitutions_dual_all_GPT-5_Mini.svg" id="A4.F46.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 46: </span>Average normalised substitutions over proposals in temporal order for GPT-5 Mini.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F47" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08098v1/substitutions_all_GPT-OSS_120B_on.svg" id="A4.F47.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/153;" width="229" height="153"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 47: </span>Average normalised substitutions over proposals in temporal order for GPT-OSS 120B.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F48" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08098v1/substitutions_all_DeepSeek_Chat_v3.1_on.svg" id="A4.F48.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/153;" width="229" height="153"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 48: </span>Average normalised substitutions over proposals in temporal order for DeepSeek Chat v3.1.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As explained at the beginning of Section <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>, some models make deals by agreeing on an increasing number of items throughout the negotiation. These models’ bargaining can be said to be coherent in this sense. Initially, in such cases of coherent bargaining, there will be high disagreement, which decreases until players agree on a final deal.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To capture this collaborative movement in the negotiation, we measure how much each proposal deviates from its predecessor. Specifically, we count the items added or removed between time <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and normalise by the size of the proposal at <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields the normalised substitutions at time <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the normalised substitution count <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the size of the symmetric difference between the proposals at <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, divided by the size of the proposal at <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th proposal:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}\;=\;\frac{\lvert S_{t}\triangle S_{t-1}\rvert}{\lvert S_{t-1}\rvert},\quad t\geq 2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">△</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}\;=\;\frac{\lvert S_{t}\triangle S_{t-1}\rvert}{\lvert S_{t-1}\rvert},\quad t\geq 2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#A4.F45" title="Figure 45 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>–<a href="#A4.F48" title="Figure 48 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a> depict average normalised substitution counts over each <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> for some selected models. In Figures <a href="#A4.F45" title="Figure 45 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F46" title="Figure 46 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>, we can again see that for our two best models (GPT-5 and GPT-5 Mini) we have higher initial disagreement with a more rapid and smooth descent when reasoning is enabled, indicating more efficient and coherent bargaining in commercial GPT models, which is amplified by enabling reasoning. DeepSeek Chat v3.1 shows the same pattern, but the initial disagreement is much lower (cf. Fig. <a href="#A4.F48" title="Figure 48 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>). The GPT-OSS substitution pattern (cf. Fig. <a href="#A4.F47" title="Figure 47 ‣ Collaboration ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) shows high volatility and hence strong willingness to change the running proposal, with high overall difference, which may tie into the earlier discussed phenomenon of stubbornness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counterpart Modelling</h5>

<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Counterpart Modelling ‣ D.5 Detailed Analysis ‣ Appendix D Air Balloon Survival - Game Details ‣ The Price of Thought: A Multilingual Analysis of Reasoning, Performance, and Cost of Negotiation in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> reports the per-instance Pareto-adherence rate, i.e. the per-instance relative frequency of proposals which lie on the Pareto front. Because Pareto-efficient proposals require aligning both agents’ pay-offs – albeit not necessarily in a balanced way – higher adherence may indicate stronger counterpart and self-modelling, i.e. the ability to infer the other player’s item valuations and adjust one’s own search for possible deals accordingly. Additionally, we observe that bargaining along the Pareto front is a strong indicator of overall performance in our game and almost perfectly separates the set of reasoning models from the set of non-reasoning models if we order them by Pareto-adherence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T13" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A4.T13.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T13.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
